# Plan design structure (Cursor-specific)

Personal flow optimization — not project documentation. Do not add this to `AGENTS.md` or feature docs.

When creating or updating a Cursor plan, write for **two audiences** and keep this order:

1. User request
2. High-level design (Problem / Solution)
3. Objective analysis
4. Low-level design
5. Commit strategy (per `plan-commit-workflow.mdc`)

| Section | Primary audience | Job |
| ------- | ---------------- | --- |
| User request + High-level + Objective analysis | **User** | Vet the design and whether the ask is worth doing |
| Low-level design (+ Commit strategy mechanics) | **Agents** | Execute the approved design |

Write each section for its primary audience. Secondary skim is fine; do not optimize user sections for agent executability, and do not treat low-level as the user’s review surface.

## User request

Short restatement of intent and in/out of scope. Not a design.

## High-level design (user-primary)

Two subsections: **Problem**, then **Solution**. Keep them cleanly split.

### User concern layers (defines breadth)

Breadth is over what the user cares about for design review — not every stack layer equally.

**Default interest** (cover when touched): UX, modeling, semantics, package-level organization.

**Usually lower interest** (assume “just works” unless the change is about them or they threaten layers above): web API wiring and similar plumbing — note *when* and *what* at a glance; other internals (SQL pipelines, serializers, etc.). Dive deeper only when implementation debt starts harming UX/modeling/semantics/package boundaries (including multi-session cleanup campaigns).

### Constraints

- **Breadth without depth (default).** Brief coverage of the full *user-relevant* width. Name every major user-relevant part; keep straightforward parts thin. Routine plumbing depth belongs in low-level.
- **Risk-scaled depth.** Go deeper where an area is troublesome, ambiguous, carries cons, or is implementation harming user-interest layers. Danger-free changes stay thin.
- **No truncated halves.** If the user-relevant domain has two large halves (e.g. modeling + UX), both appear in Problem and Solution. Do not require a full tour of lower-interest layers — a one-line API note is enough when incidental.
- **Little/no code** unless pivotal to the design decision. Prefer concepts, boundaries, and how parts fit together.
- **Vettable without low-level.** User request + High-level + Objective analysis must be enough to approve or reject.
- Scale depth by **risk and user concern**, not stack completeness.

## Objective analysis (user-primary)

After high-level, before low-level. Critical appraisal of the **request** and proposed changes — not a sales pitch.

- Pros, cons (**when real**), and value/worth (is this worth the cost/risk?).
- **Run the check; don’t force the shape.** Outcome may range from short “looks sound” to strong “back up.” Do not invent cons for balance theater; do not omit pushback when warranted.
- **Pushback is extra free here:** wrong problem, weak value, better framing, shrink scope, or “do not do this as asked.” Candor over agreeableness.
- Decision support, not an undecided Option A vs B menu. Solution stays the concrete approach unless analysis says refuse or reframe — then say so clearly.
- Real cons cue deeper high-level Solution detail on those areas.

## Low-level design (agent-primary)

Files, symbols, steps, tests. Routine granularity lives here. Problem framing stays light. Todos and verify/commit gating live here (with commit strategy). Do not let this section replace user-facing breadth, analysis, or risk-focused high-level depth.

## Anti-patterns

- Writing the whole plan as if only agents will read it
- Leading with file lists or “change X then Y” before Problem/Solution
- Omitting a user-relevant half while expanding lower-interest plumbing
- Forcing equal high-level depth across incidental stack layers
- Making every high-level part granular when the change is mostly straightforward
- Skipping Objective analysis, or cheerleading with no real cons/value/pushback when warranted
- Inventing cons or padding a beefy Cons section when few real downsides exist — the check matters; “everything looks fine” is valid when earned
- Mixing problem diagnosis into Solution or Low-level without a Problem subsection
- Treating “brief” as license to omit a major user-relevant part or hide a known con
